{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's causal mechanism is circular with respect to the problem's stipulation. The problem asks to compare a decentralized network against a monolith 'with strictly more total internal redundancy' and argue which is more resilient to correlated failure. But the target's mechanism for decentralization's superior resilience is that the decentralized services 'avoid a shared operational substrate' via structural heterogeneity. A monolith with strictly more total internal redundancy could itself deploy exactly that same mechanism internally—diversified substrates, heterogeneous library implementations, geographically isolated redundancy domains—so the target's claimed distinguishing causal factor (heterogeneity) is an orthogonal design choice, not a property that separates decentralization from a monolith. The target never establishes why a decentralized network necessarily has MORE substrate diversity than a monolith that is stipulated to have strictly more total internal redundancy, so it fails to argue the claim against the problem's stated comparator.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}